<title>USA: High Plains Wheat - HRW crops face freezing weekend.</title>
<headline>High Plains Wheat - HRW crops face freezing weekend.</headline>
<dateline>KANSAS CITY, Mo. 1997-04-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Concern about possible damage to the hard red winter wheat crop this weekend continued to grow Friday with freezing temperatures forecast.</p>
<p>&quot;Northern Kansas will survive without too much damage but southern Kansas, Oklahoma and Kansas could have a lot of damage. The wheat is already jointed,&quot; one Kansas dealer said.</p>
<p>Parts of Oklahoma, however, had heavy showers early Friday which could help to lessen the impact of the cold weather. Moisture helps to keep soil warm.</p>
<p>&quot;It does look bleak but the rain could save us,&quot; an Oklahoma dealer said.</p>
<p>One dealer in Kansas pushed his bid five cents and bought a substantial amount of both old and new crop wheat. Most dealers, however, said business was slow.</p>
<p>Further north in southwest Nebraska temperatures dropped as low of 14 degrees Fahrenheit early Friday.</p>
<p>&quot;Our wheat is not as far along as it is in Kansas and Oklahoma, which should help,&quot; one Nebraska dealer said.  </p>
